  "requirement": "The Habeas Codicem book MUST configure mdBook's Git repository link for `nikomatsakis/habeas-codicem` and its page edit links for the `main` branch.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**The configuration is correct, but nothing in the project links it to the requirement.**\n\n- **Behavior:** `book.toml` lines 11–16, in `[output.html]`, set `git-repository-url = \"https://github.com/nikomatsakis/habeas-codicem\"`. They also set `edit-url-template = \"https://github.com/nikomatsakis/habeas-codicem/edit/main/{path}\"`. That is the repository link for `nikomatsakis/habeas-codicem` and the edit links for the `main` branch, as the requirement states.\n- **No `{impl}` reference:** The `references` lookup for `requirements.layout.repository_links` returns no sites. The requirement in `md/requirements/layout.md` has no `:impl:` anchor either. `book.toml` does mark other requirements, for example `{impl}` for `requirements.cli.mdbook` at line 6. The `[output.html]` table that implements this requirement carries no marker. Under the assessment rules, code that implements a requirement without an `{impl}` reference is a gap.\n- **No `{test}` reference:** No test or anchor verifies that the book keeps these settings. The related tests in `src/index.rs`, such as `source_links_use_configured_repository_and_branch`, are marked for `requirements.index.source_links`. They use synthetic example configs and never read the project's `book.toml`. The requirement is simple, but a small test or a `:test:` anchor on `book.toml` would still be easy and is missing.\n\nThe fix is to mark the `[output.html]` repository settings in `book.toml` with `{impl}` for this requirement. A `{test}` that checks the real `book.toml` values should be added too.",
